 Democratic lead in a district that leans Democratic by three points amid a favorable national climate. Little's fundraising edge and outside support help offset the loss of incumbency with Craig retiring.", "watch": "Tightening in late polling, a surge of Republican outside spending, or scrutiny of Little's progressive profile in swing suburbs could narrow the race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Angie Craig (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Angie Craig's retirement leaves this southern Twin Cities suburbs seat open, with Democrat Matt Little facing Republican Eric Pratt. The district leans Democratic by about three points, and national conditions in 2026 favor Democrats. Little won a contested primary in August and has raised more than Pratt, with outside groups adding support on his side, though Pratt holds more cash on hand heading into the fall.\n\nRaters largely call the race Likely Democratic, with several placing it Safe or Solid. A September poll sponsored by Democratic groups showed Little ahead by twelve points; an earlier Republican-sponsored poll had a five-point margin. Prediction markets put Democratic chances above ninety percent. Little enters the fall favored by roughly eleven to twelve points, with enough residual uncertainty that the seat falls short of safe.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9565, "margin": 11.37}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9477, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9516, "margin": 11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9571, "margin": 11.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9414, "margin": 11.5}]}